    "result_text": "The build passes with the test green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule \"reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date \u2192 400\" by following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, keeping each piece in its own single-responsibility unit:\n\n**New files:**\n- `RequireRegistrationDateNotFuture.java` \u2014 a `Require<X>` step that reads the built owner's supplied `registrationDate` and throws when it's non-null and after `LocalDate.now()`. It runs *before* `ResolveOwnerRegistrationDate` so it validates the value as supplied; an omitted date (later defaulted to the server date) passes through untouched.\n- `FutureRegistrationDateException.java` \u2014 a checked exception (mirrors the existing `InvalidPostcodeException`/`CityAtCapacityException` pattern).\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `400 BAD_REQUEST` via the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper.\n- `...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml` \u2014 the escalation binding.\n\n**Wiring:** inserted the `requireRegistrationDateNotFuture` step into `owners.POST.yml` between `assignHouseholdSize` and `resolveRegistrationDate`, with the same `govern: [ transaction ]` as its neighbours.\n\nI reused existing infrastructure throughout (`ProblemDetails`, the exception/handler/escalation-yml triad, the `@Val Owner` state hand-off) rather than duplicating logic. `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
